WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:06.330
[HAITIAN CREOLE SPEECH]:: Anpil
nan kesyon ke nou te poze nan

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.858
kou a te baze sou biyografi
lengwistik etidyan yo.

00:00:08.858 --> 00:00:11.400
Li te vreman enteresan ni pou
etidyan yo ni pou mwen menm kòm

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:11.900
anseyan.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.160
SARAH HANSEN: Today on
the podcast, what language

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.780
teaches us about identity.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.960
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Can you
be Mauritian but not

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.610
speak the Mauritian
Creole language?

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:27.840
Can you be Latino but
not be fluent in Spanish?

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.360
Can you be African
American but yet not be

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.548
fully fluent in
so-called Black English?

00:00:34.548 --> 00:00:36.090
SARAH HANSEN: Welcome
to Chalk Radio,

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.820
a podcast about inspired
teaching at MIT.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:44.280
I'm your host, Sarah Hansen,
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.710
My guest for this
episode created a course

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.600
that teaches students
about how language

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.240
helps us see and better
understand our world.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.080
Michel DeGraff teaches the
course Creole Languages

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.090
and Caribbean Identities.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.280
The course highlights how
linguistics can teach us

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.420
about broader issues of
identity, history, education,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:04.620
and more.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.030
Throughout this
episode, we'll be

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.220
intercutting professor
DeGraff's answers

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.350
with his Creole translations.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.140
For now, we'll let him
explain more about how he

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.650
developed this unique course.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.760
[HAITIAN CREOLE SPEECH]:: Rechèch
mwen se sou devlòpman avèk

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.600
MICHEL DEGRAFF: My
research is about

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.720
trying to understand how
languages evolve over time

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.160
and what happens when new
languages are created.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.670
Say, for example, in the
Caribbean in my native Haiti,

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.610
where the Haitian Creole emerged
in the 17th-18th century.

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.270
So we try to understand
the patterns of development

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.320
of the things that we
call Creole languages.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.300
In terms of the structure, but
also an applied side to my work

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.710
where I try to understand
how best to use, say,

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:49.620
Haitian Creole and other
local languages in education.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.110
And how that's crucial
for development

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.850
of countries like Haiti.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.190
So the course started
out of a desire

00:01:56.190 --> 00:02:00.450
to try to have linguistics
be accessible to a broader

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.960
range of students, within which
we could connect languages

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.900
to larger issues of
culture and identity.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.423
And as I'm from Haiti and as my
research is Creole languages,

00:02:11.423 --> 00:02:12.840
I try to think of
ways in which we

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.050
can use the history
of Creole languages

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:19.140
to introduce larger issues
connected to linguistics,

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.590
identity, history,
sociology, education.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:25.090
SARAH HANSEN: Even
though the course

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:28.810
focuses on Caribbean identities
and Creole languages,

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.060
the course requires
its students who

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.760
come from all over the world
to observe what language

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.227
means for their own identities.

00:02:36.227 --> 00:02:38.560
[HAITIAN CREOLE SPEECH]:: Se
te [?] moun Anpil yo fèt oz

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:39.060
Etazini

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.360
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Can you be
Mauritian but yet not speak

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:42.610
the Mauritian Creole language?

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:46.810
Can you be Latino but
not be fluent in Spanish?

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:49.360
Can you be African
American but yet not be

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.990
fully fluent in
so-called Black English?

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:55.870
So those were questions that
they themselves brought in.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:59.080
Then what I ask them to do as
the very first exercise was

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:02.320
to write what I called
a linguistic biography.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.810
And actually, I got this
hint from a dear friend

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:08.290
and colleague, Professor
Anne Charity Hudley,

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:10.150
and Charity actually
shared with me

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:11.532
her own linguistic biography.

00:03:11.532 --> 00:03:13.240
When I read that, I
realized how powerful

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:16.900
it was to read about her
own growing up in terms

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.900
of language, ethnicity, race.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.998
So I thought that I would ask
students something similar.

00:03:22.998 --> 00:03:25.290
SARAH HANSEN: The connection
between language and power

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.310
is highlighted in this course.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:30.270
A lot of what comes through in
students' language biographies

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.970
and other assignments
touches on power structures

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.950
and the hierarchies
that exist because

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:39.180
of cultural misconceptions
tied to language and also race.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:42.180
I asked Professor DeGraff if
students brought misconceptions

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.760
about Creole languages
to the course,

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.760
and how he unpacked them.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.960
[HAITIAN CREOLE SPEECH]::
Etidyan yo,

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:51.370
yo kòmanse kou a avèk yon se...

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:53.170
MICHEL DEGRAFF:
So one way that we

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:58.480
start by unpacking these
misconceptions is by comparing

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.760
attitudes towards
languages with attitudes

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.190
towards race and ethnicity.

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:05.950
And what we get to
see very quickly

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:08.510
is that, in fact,
there is perhaps

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:10.420
as much misconceptions
about language

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:12.940
as there is about race.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:15.400
So they get to see very
early in the course

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.519
that language can be
used as a proxy for race,

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:23.110
though now it's impolite to
say certain things about, say,

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:26.110
black people or Asian people.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:30.280
Yet it's still polite to say
negative things about language.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:31.950
And then at that
point what we do,

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:35.440
we introduce some basic
linguistic analysis

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:38.200
tools for them to be
able to see that in terms

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.840
of basic structure,
there is nothing

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:43.968
at all that is inferior
about Creole languages.

00:04:43.968 --> 00:04:45.760
In fact, what we do
from the very beginning

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:47.890
is to show that
structurally there

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:50.900
are patterns both in the
syntax or morphology of Creole

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:54.070
languages that are quite
similar to patterns

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:57.400
in the syntax and history
of English, of French,

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:59.890
of Mandarin Chinese.

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:03.980
So they quickly get a sense that
empirically and theoretically,

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:08.470
one cannot argue that Creole
languages are any way lesser

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:11.170
than non-Creole languages,
the same way that there are no

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:15.070
biological measures that
would deem black people to be

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:16.700
inferior to white people.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:18.730
So they get very
quick to understand

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:21.610
the power of this myth
that often they themselves

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:23.590
have brought along
in terms of how

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:25.265
they view different languages.

00:05:25.265 --> 00:05:27.140
In some cases Creole
language, in other cases

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:28.680
just even dialects of English.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:30.760
There is Southern
English or Black English.

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:32.590
Their attitudes
are very demeaning

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:36.280
towards those varieties that
have no basis in science that

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:38.440
are rooted in
hierarchies of power,

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:41.230
and they get to see
that very quickly.

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:44.140
This course, the core
of it is discussions.

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:47.390
And in order for the
course to be successful,

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:52.450
we have to be able to discuss
very delicate personal issues

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:54.760
in a very safe way
and to be honest.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:57.690
It cannot be that students
are afraid to reveal

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:01.850
their positions on various
topics including race,

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:03.390
language, identity.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.830
And one way to do
it is to, given

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:07.878
that I had at the very
beginning of the course--

00:06:07.878 --> 00:06:10.045
I had the questionnaires
about where they came from,

00:06:10.045 --> 00:06:12.940
what languages they spoke,
where they grew up--

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:16.930
I was able to use that
to trigger discussions.

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:20.260
So if we read a text about
authenticity, for example.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:22.910
If an African-- can you
be an authentic writer

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:24.580
if you write in
English, for example?

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:27.730
I knew that some of the students
had dealt with this issue.

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:29.920
For example, we
had a student who,

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:32.680
although she was
black and Hispanic,

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:35.350
she wasn't very
fluent in Spanish.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:39.520
And she wasn't clear whether
her not being fluent in Spanish

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:41.210
made her any less Hispanic.

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:41.710
You see?

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:45.610
So I was able to use that, in
order to trigger a discussion

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:48.850
around this notion that we
had read in this famous debate

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:54.220
between Chinua Achebe and
Ngugi wa Thiong'o about African

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:55.060
writers.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:57.450
Can a writer be
authentically African

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.450
if they write in English,
or French for that matter?

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:04.400
So that was one way to
trigger rich discussion.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:07.800
So the motive there was
which discussions are best

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:11.030
arrived at if we start
from a personal basis

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:14.980
where students are invested in
trying to find an answer that

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:18.070
can work for them in
their personal lives or in

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:20.260
the life of their families,
their communities,

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:22.708
their countries, et cetera.

00:07:22.708 --> 00:07:24.250
SARAH HANSEN: Another
special feature

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:26.840
of Professor DeGraff's
course on OCW

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:30.410
is that his Instructor Insights
are available in both English

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:32.120
and Haitian Creole.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:35.910
I asked him why it was important
that he share in both languages

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:37.627
how the course was taught.

00:07:37.627 --> 00:07:39.960
[HAITIAN CREOLE SPEECH]:: Pou
mwen, se te vreman enpòtan

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:41.360
MICHEL DEGRAFF:
So it was very, I

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:45.530
would say, crucial, for me to do
this interview in both English

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:50.750
and Creole, because I see
Creole languages, even

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:53.600
in the case of Haiti, or
especially because of Haiti,

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:58.700
as a particular revealing
and clear test tube or case

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:01.760
study to really show
how language can

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:06.170
be such a powerful tool,
either for domination

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:07.160
or for resistance.

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:10.340
Either for oppression
or for development.

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:13.400
So I would love teachers
who look at the course

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:16.040
to see how they can adapt
the content in order

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:19.310
to address similar issues
in their own community,

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:21.260
because I think that
what you see happening

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:23.930
in the case of Creole languages
also happens in the US,

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:27.260
happens in Europe, in
Latin America, in Africa--

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:32.240
where language is often used as
this technology for domination

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:33.950
or resistance.

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:37.940
And I think this course gives
some basic analytical tools

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:40.520
to be able to analyze
these patterns.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:43.159
And for me, it's
also very important

00:08:43.159 --> 00:08:46.690
that teachers in Haiti and
students in Haiti including,

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:49.850
say, future leaders
or policy makers,

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:53.420
get to understand the
discourse and the readings

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:55.970
and to understand that
Haiti could do so much

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:59.150
better if teachers,
students, leaders,

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:03.050
had this awareness of how their
own attitudes towards Creole

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:07.490
versus French have played
over centuries in keeping

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:08.630
the country underdeveloped.

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:12.590
And if they could get a
better sense of the importance

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:15.148
of language in education
and development,

00:09:15.148 --> 00:09:17.190
then I think the country
would do so much better.

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:20.420
And for me, it's
a key bottleneck

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:22.220
that we have to go
past in order to really

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:26.180
be able to develop a nation--
is to be able to understand

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:29.440
the history and the
nature of your national--

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:32.210
your ancestral language-- and
to be able to use it in a more

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:33.630
systematic and productive way.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:36.047
If you cannot do that, there's
no way that you can develop

00:09:36.047 --> 00:09:38.492
a whole nation that
speaks that language.

00:09:38.492 --> 00:09:39.950
What the students
get to understand

00:09:39.950 --> 00:09:42.440
at the end of the
course is that we all

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:45.140
hold dear certain
attitudes about language.

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:47.400
And some of these attitudes
are positive for ourselves

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:49.687
and for others, but oftentimes--
and this is actually

00:09:49.687 --> 00:09:52.270
one of the major issues that we
discuss throughout the course,

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:54.140
is that sometimes
we have attitudes

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:57.890
that have a negative impact,
severe negative impact,

00:09:57.890 --> 00:09:59.630
on the lives of other people.

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:02.892
The lives of, sometimes,
entire communities and nations.

00:10:02.892 --> 00:10:04.850
So I hope throughout that
students can actually

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:07.965
take the theory that
is in the course

00:10:07.965 --> 00:10:09.590
and see ways in which
they can apply it

00:10:09.590 --> 00:10:11.780
to themselves, to
their communities,

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:13.100
to their countries.

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:15.950
So some in the
course of this class

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:18.140
have applied what
they've learned,

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:22.130
and taking it outside, including
volunteering in, say, there

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:25.345
is a dual language program
that's just started in Boston.

00:10:25.345 --> 00:10:28.010
It's a joint language
program promoting

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:30.860
the use of both
Creole and English,

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:34.130
and where students are
immersed from kindergarten

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:36.170
in both Creole and English.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:38.880
And now there's a student
within the class, my class,

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:42.530
who's volunteering in that
classroom for kindergartners

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:46.160
who are learning in
Creole and in English.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:48.080
And to me, that's a--

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:51.080
it's a dream, because
it's one case where

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:54.500
what you learn in the course
can be directly applied

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:57.600
in the real world context, which
can make actual positive change

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:58.850
in the life of these children.

00:11:03.443 --> 00:11:05.860
SARAH HANSEN: Professor DeGraff
shares additional thoughts

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:07.930
on teaching Creole
languages and Caribbean

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:09.970
identities within the
instructor insight

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:12.610
section of his OCW course.

00:11:12.610 --> 00:11:14.770
You can find these
dual language videos,

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:17.290
along with his openly
licensed teaching materials,

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:20.470
at ocw.mit.edu.

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:22.990
And if you use these materials
in your own teaching,

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:24.550
we'd love to hear about it.

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:28.060
Use the contact link in the
show notes to share your story.

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:33.690
Until next time, I'm Sarah
Hansen from MIT OpenCourseWare.

